+## 5. `repeat ... until` written on one line is not recognised, and the counted form's count may not span lines
+
+**Severity: minor. A limit of the disambiguation, not a defect in what is
+shipped.**
+
+`repeat` opens two forms, and `Parser::opens_post_test_loop`
+(`src/parser.rs:1006`) tells them apart by a newline after the keyword:
+`repeat <expression> times` is one line, and `repeat` alone opens a body an
+`until` ends. Looking for the `until` instead does not work — it is at the *end*
+of the body, and a counted loop whose body holds a post-test loop of its own has
+one too, at a nesting level the parser is nowhere near yet.
+
+The cost is that
+
+```
+repeat set n to n + 1 until n is 3
+```
+
+is read as the counted form and refused. The shape `SPEC.md:432` and
+`docs/GRAMMAR.md:246` show is the multi-line one, and that is what parses and
+what the REPL, the formatter, both engines and every test in this phase use.
+Making the one-line spelling work means deciding whether the counted form's
+count may itself span lines, which is a language-surface question rather than a
+parser bug, so it is recorded here rather than decided in a bug-fix phase.
\ No newline at end of file

